+# The P2MR rescue lane: elliptic curve checks of leaf version 0xe0 rescued with a proof of seed knowledge
+
+This build adds a rescue lane to P2MR leaves of leaf version `0xe0` ([p2mr-tripwire.md](p2mr-tripwire.md)).
+From a set height on, an elliptic curve signature check in such a leaf whose signature element is a
+**rescue token** is checked by verifying a VOLE-in-the-head proof, carried in the input's annex, that
+the spender knows the seed the key was derived from, without revealing the seed. It is part M4-R1 of
+the M4 series of the Ark-0 test signet. It is not part of BIP 360 or BIP 361, it is not proposed for
+Bitcoin, and it does not make any network quantum-resistant.
+
+The proof system is that of ark0-rescue `SPEC.md` v0.2 revision 2 (version byte `0x02`), statement
+`0x01`: knowledge of `x` with `HMAC-SHA256(K, x || suffix) = y`. Its security rests on an argument in the
+classical random oracle model with a 128-bit target; its security against quantum attackers is
+conjectured, not proven, and it has had no external review.
+
+**It relaxes the rules: a hard fork.** A leaf such as `<P> OP_CHECKSIG` frozen by the tripwire has no
+valid witness under the earlier rules; the lane makes some valid. A node without this build refuses
+the first block that contains a rescue spend and stops there (see [Activation](#activation)).
+
+**Not active on Ark-0.** The height for the Ark-0 signet, `ARK0_RESCUE_HEIGHT`, is not decided: until
+it is, it is `std::numeric_limits<int>::max()` and the lane stays closed there. Regtest and custom
+signets given `-signetrescue` have it.
+
+**Publishing a rescue spend publishes the key.** The token carries `y`, which is the Schnorr secret key
+`d` of the spent output. While elliptic curve checks in the leaf work, anyone who sees a rescue spend
+can spend the same output with an ordinary signature; once the checks are disabled, the output's
+safety depends on them staying disabled, that is, on no reorganization undoing the tripwire spend.
+The mempool takes rescue spends only when the checks are disabled for the next block (see
+[Policy](#policy)); the rule itself does not depend on it.
+
+## Where the lane applies
+
+A check is a rescue check when all of these hold, rule R1:
+
+- it is `OP_CHECKSIG`, `OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY` or `OP_CHECKSIGADD` with a 32-byte public key and a
+  non-empty signature element;
+- it executes in a P2MR leaf of version `0xe0` at depth one or more, with
+  `SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_TRIPWIRE` (the leaf executes);
+- `SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_NO_RESCUE` does not apply (the lane is open for the block);
+- the signature element is a well-formed rescue token.
+
+This is decided before the tripwire rule of [p2mr-tripwire.md](p2mr-tripwire.md) (rule 5 there), and
+whatever the state of elliptic curve checks: from the height on, a token is a rescue check whether
+the checks are disabled or not. Every other check is as before: with the checks disabled it fails
+with `P2MR_EC_DISABLED`, otherwise it is a BIP 340 check. Leaf version `0xc0`, P2TR, legacy and
+witness v0 scripts, `OP_CHECKMLDSA44` and `OP_CHECKSLHDSA128S` are not affected.
+
+`SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_NO_RESCUE` restricts: with it, a token is a signature like any other, which can
+never be a valid one. It is set for every block below the lane's height, and is in neither
+`STANDARD_SCRIPT_VERIFY_FLAGS` nor `MANDATORY_SCRIPT_VERIFY_FLAGS`, so that adding a flag only ever
+makes fewer spends valid.
+
+## Encoding
+
+```
+token = 0x03 || sid || pub         pub = u8(|K|) || K || u8(suffix) || y     (SPEC.md section 7.5)
+annex = 0x50 || 0x01 || proof      proof: 130,218 bytes for statement 0x01   (SPEC.md section 10)
+```
+
+An element is a token exactly when: its first byte is `0x03`; `sid` is a registered statement id
+(only `0x01`); `1 <= |K| <= 64`, every byte of `K` in `0x20..0x7e`; `y` is 32 bytes and nothing
+follows; and the element is not 64 or 65 bytes long, so `|K|` is neither 28 nor 29. The last
+condition keeps every element that can be a valid BIP 340 signature from being a token. A token is
+`|K| + 36` bytes; for the Ark-0 web wallet v1, `K = "ark0-wallet v1 schnorr"`, 58 bytes. There is
+no error for a malformed token: it is not a token.
+
+The annex is exactly one record: `0x50`, the record type `0x01` ("P2MR rescue proof v1") and the
+proof, with no length prefix. Spends that make no rescue check keep the annex semantics of BIP 341.
+
+## Execution
+
+In this order, after the 50 of BIP 342 for a non-empty signature and the public key size check:
+
+| Rule | | Error |
+|---|---|---|
+| R2 | charge `VALIDATION_WEIGHT_PER_P2MR_RESCUE_CHECK` before any hashing or parsing of the annex | `TAPSCRIPT_VALIDATION_WEIGHT` |
+| R3 | at most one rescue check per input, counted before the signature cache is looked at | `P2MR_RESCUE_REPEAT` |
+| R4 | the input's annex is exactly one rescue record for the token's statement | `P2MR_RESCUE_ANNEX` |
+| R5 | `0 < y < n` and `x(y*G)` is the check's key, either parity (libsecp256k1) | `P2MR_RESCUE_KEY` |
+| R6 | the rescue signature message (below); missing transaction data fails as `MissingDataBehavior` says | `P2MR_RESCUE_PROOF` |
+| R7 | `voleith_verify_s1(K, suffix, y, m, proof)` with `m` of exactly 96 bytes; any error in the library is a rejection | `P2MR_RESCUE_PROOF` |
+| R8 | otherwise the check passes as a valid signature does: `OP_CHECKSIG` pushes true, `OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY` goes on, `OP_CHECKSIGADD` adds 1 | |
+
+Because of R3, a leaf with two checks of keys that need rescuing (a 2-of-2, for example) cannot be
+rescued.
+
+### Rescue signature message
+
+```
+rescue_sighash = TaggedHash("P2MR/Rescue/Sighash",
+      0x00 (epoch) || 0x00 (hash type: SIGHASH_DEFAULT only)
+   || nVersion || nLockTime
+   || sha_prevouts || sha_amounts || sha_scriptpubkeys || sha_sequences || sha_outputs
+   || 0x03 (spend type: ext_flag 1, annex present) || u32le(input index)
+   || tapleaf_hash || 0x03 (key_version) || u32le(codesep_pos)
+   || network_id || sid || SHA256(pub))
+network_id     = SHA256(signet challenge) on a signet, SHA256("regtest") on regtest
+pk_commitment  = TaggedHash("P2MR/Rescue/Key", 0x00 || P)
+m              = rescue_sighash || network_id || pk_commitment
+```
+
+The message commits to everything a BIP 341 `SIGHASH_ALL` message does, but not to the annex,
+which holds the proof. Like a BIP 341 signature, it does not commit to the other inputs' scriptSigs,
+to the control block, or to witness elements the script does not check: a third party can change
+those and leave the transaction valid, changing its wtxid (and, for a scriptSig, its txid). Other
+signatures in the same input commit to the whole annex, proof included, as BIP 341 says.
+
+### Signature cache
+
+The result of R7 is cached under a fifth salted hasher (padding byte `R`), keyed by
+`m || sid || pub || proof`. R1 to R6 run before the cache is looked at; only the proof verification
+is saved.
+
+## Verification library
+
+The verifier is the Rust crate `voleith` of ark0-rescue, vendored with its dependencies in
+`src/rescue/` (see its README for the source commit, every file's SHA-256 and the licences) and
+built by CMake into a static library. The build uses one exact toolchain (Rust 1.97.0), run directly
+rather than through rustup, with no cargo or Rust variable of the environment and no cargo
+configuration file. It uses the crate's release profile (`panic = "unwind"`, which the C ABI needs;
+the build checks it) and no default features, which leaves the prover out, and remaps every local
+path. Any change to a vendored file rebuilds the library from nothing. It is linked into
+`bitcoin_consensus` and `bitcoinkernel`. A shared `bitcoinkernel` exports none of its symbols; a
+static one is installed with it as `libbitcoinkernel_voleith`, which `pkg-config --static` lists with
+the system libraries it needs. As with upstream's static kernel, a program links it with a C++ compiler
+driver, which adds the C++ runtime. Its C ABI is one function, `voleith_verify_s1`
+(`src/rescue/voleith/include/voleith.h`), which checks every length before it reads a pointer,
+returns 0 for any malformed input, and catches panics. Building the node needs `cargo` and `rustc`
+1.97.0; the crate's own prover is built for the functional tests only.
+
+## Validation weight
+
+`VALIDATION_WEIGHT_PER_P2MR_RESCUE_CHECK` is **100,000, provisional**: it is fixed by measurement
+before release, by a single-check condition
+`C >= 1.5 x 50 x p99(rescue check) / median(Schnorr check)` over the most expensive invalid proof, a
+65,535-byte message and a valid proof, and by a whole-block condition. Whatever it is fixed at, it
+must not exceed 130,333: the smallest valid rescue input (`|K| = 1`, template R1 at depth one) has a
+witness of 130,333 bytes and a budget of 130,383, which has to pay for the 50 and the rescue weight.
+A second rescue check in the same input never fits the budget of an input that carries one proof.
+
+## Activation
+
+| Network | Lane open from |
+|---|---|
+| mainnet, testnet3, testnet4, default signet | never |
+| Ark-0 signet | `ARK0_RESCUE_HEIGHT`, not decided: never in this build |
+| other custom signets | `-signetrescue=<height>`, with `-signetchallenge` and `-signettripwire`, at or above the tripwire height |
+| regtest | the final `p2mr_tripwire` height (after `-testactivationheight` and `-signettripwire`), or `-testactivationheight=p2mr_rescue@<height>`, at or above it |
+
+`p2mr_rescue` is a buried deployment. `GetBlockScriptFlags` sets `SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_NO_RESCUE` for
+every block below the height. `-signetrescue` is refused on Ark-0, on the default signet and on the
+other chains; regtest refuses an explicit height below the final tripwire height. A node logs at
+startup:
+
+```
+Signet P2MR rescue lane from height <H> for elliptic curve checks disabled by the tripwire (VOLEitH v0.2, statement 0x01); network id <hex>
+```
+
+The lane adds no state: whether a rescue spend is valid depends only on the block's height, whether
+the leaf executes, and the transaction. Restarts, `-reindex`, pruning and assumeutxo have nothing to
+restore.
+
+**The hard fork.** A node without this build (an M3 or M4-S node) finds the first block with a rescue
+spend invalid, marks it so, and stops: on a network whose blocks one party signs, it has no other
+chain to follow, as long as that party extends only one chain. Upgraded later, it still has the block
+marked invalid: `bitcoin-cli reconsiderblock <hash of that block>` lets it continue.
+
+## Policy
+
+1. The mempool checks with `SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_NO_RESCUE` until the lane is open **at the tip itself**,
+   not just for the next block: `ConsensusScriptChecks` checks again with the tip's own flags, which
+   keep the restriction below the height. The first rescue spend through the mempool goes in the
+   block after the first open one; one in the first open block has to be submitted directly, with
+   `generateblock` for example.
+2. A transaction with a rescue record is taken only when elliptic curve checks in leaf version `0xe0`
+   are disabled for the next block, from the confirmed UTXO set (`p2mr-rescue-ec-enabled` otherwise).
+   This is a policy check, not a script flag. Spends already in the mempool stay there when a
+   reorganization enables the checks again; spends from a disconnected block are refused if the
+   checks then work.
+3. The one standard template is R1: a leaf of version `0xe0` at depth one or more whose script is
+   `<32-byte key> OP_CHECKSIG`, byte for byte, and a witness of exactly the token, the script, the
+   control block and the annex. Its token is exempt from the 80-byte limit (100 bytes at most), and
+   its annex is allowed; every other annex stays non-standard. Other uses of the lane are valid in
+   blocks but not relayed. A standard transaction holds at most three rescue inputs.
+4. When the tip goes back below the lane's height, transactions with a rescue record are removed,
+   with their descendants.
+5. Over P2P, a transaction with a rescue record is taken only from a peer with `noban` permission and
+   dropped otherwise, before validation and before the orphan pool, without penalty and without the
+   reject filters; its txid and wtxid both count as answered, so another peer is asked at once.
+   `-whitelist` applies to inbound peers only unless it names a direction: use
+   `-whitelist=noban,in,out@<address>` between the network's own nodes. `sendrawtransaction` and
+   `testmempoolaccept` are not limited by this.
+
+"A transaction with a rescue record" is one structural test, shared by items 2, 4 and 5: an input
+whose witness has at least two elements, the last of which starts with `0x50 0x01`. It reads no coin.
+
+## RPC and logs
+
+`gettripwireinfo` has a `rescue` object where the lane has a height: `height`, `active_next_block`,
+`mempool_accepts` (open at the tip and elliptic curve checks disabled for the next block),
+`network_id` and `statements` (`["01"]`). `getdeploymentinfo` lists `p2mr_rescue`. When a block joins
+the active chain, each rescue spend in it is logged:
+
+```
+P2MR rescue of <txid:vout> by <txid> in block <hash> (height <N>): statement 01, key <P>
+```
+
+Only the rescue spends through template R1 are logged, in a block checked with P2MR, leaf version
+`0xe0` and the lane in force (its script flags, not the deployment alone): an input that spends a
+P2MR output and whose whole witness is a token, `<P> OP_CHECKSIG` in a leaf of version `0xe0` at depth one or more, the
+control block and one rescue record. A token is never a valid BIP 340 signature, so in a valid block
+such an input went through the rescue path; finding it costs a constant amount per input and no
+elliptic curve operation. Rescue checks in other leaf shapes (`OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY`, `OP_CHECKSIGADD`,
+after `OP_CODESEPARATOR`, or with more elements), valid in a block but not relayed, are not logged.
+
+There is no RPC to make proofs or rescue transactions: proofs are made off the node.
+
+## Tests
+
+- `src/test/p2mr_rescue_tests.cpp`: the parameters of every network, the block flags at the height,
+  the token and annex encodings, the key relation, the signature message against an independent
+  implementation (`test/functional/test_framework/p2mr_rescue.py`), every rule and its failures, the
+  three flags in all combinations, the signature cache, the flag monotonicity with every prevout,
+  and the relay template. Its vectors, `src/test/data/p2mr_rescue_vectors.json`, were made once by
+  `test/util/generate_p2mr_rescue_vectors.py` with the crate's prover.
+- `test/functional/feature_p2mr_rescue.py` (regtest) and `feature_p2mr_rescue_signet.py` (a custom
+  signet): activation, the mempool, reorganizations, the P2P gate, the hard fork against an M4-S
+  node (`M4S_BITCOIND=<path to its bitcoind>`), restarts and pruning.
+- Fuzz targets `p2mr_rescue_token`, `p2mr_rescue_verify`, `p2mr_rescue` and
+  `p2mr_rescue_script_flags`, whose seeds `test/util/p2mr_rescue_fuzz_seeds.py` writes from the
+  vectors.
+- `bench_bitcoin -filter='.*P2MRRescue.*'`: the verifier, valid and in its most expensive invalid
+  form, and the whole path of a check.
